WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.691 --> 00:00:17.674
Welcome to my new smash hit, Superstars in History.

2
00:00:18.835 --> 00:00:25.760
I'm your host Faye Moss—the only talk show host in the world with access to history's biggest stars.

3
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.564
Each week I interview people from the past who've changed history in unique ways.

4
00:00:32.268 --> 00:00:36.849
They love sharing their personal stories with me—oh, and with you too!

5
00:00:37.509 --> 00:00:42.571
You're going to be absolutely fascinated by my conversation with today's guest.

6
00:00:45.251 --> 00:00:51.233
He started a famous company, dramatically left it, and made a huge comeback later on.

7
00:00:51.653 --> 00:00:56.254
He's a computer pioneer who changed the world again and again.

8
00:00:56.935 --> 00:01:01.816
Straight from history, here's the founder of Apple, Steve Jobs!

9
00:01:18.892 --> 00:01:19.472
Hello, Faye!

10
00:01:19.953 --> 00:01:20.753
Hi, Steve!

11
00:01:21.334 --> 00:01:24.456
Thank you for being my guest on Superstars in History.

12
00:01:25.016 --> 00:01:28.899
Kids today will never know a world without smartphones or tablets.

13
00:01:29.599 --> 00:01:33.422
I certainly don't know what I'd do without all my smartphones and tablets!

14
00:01:33.902 --> 00:01:36.544
But you didn't have these devices when you were a kid.

15
00:01:37.424 --> 00:01:39.446
Tell me, what was your childhood like?

16
00:01:40.566 --> 00:01:42.888
Well, I had a pretty normal childhood.

17
00:01:43.288 --> 00:01:48.031
I was born in 1955 and grew up in California, in a place that would become known as

18
00:01:48.071 --> 00:01:48.792
Silicon Valley.

19
00:01:50.108 --> 00:01:54.451
I hung out a lot with my dad, who loved working with his hands, building things and fixing

20
00:01:54.511 --> 00:02:00.634
cars. He wasn't great with electronics, but he knew the basics from working on cars and taught me.

21
00:02:01.634 --> 00:02:06.117
Did your dad teach you any secrets that helped you succeed?

22
00:02:07.318 --> 00:02:11.660
I don't know if they were secrets, Faye, but my dad thought craftsmanship was really important.

23
00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:18.003
Whenever he built something like a fence or a cabinet, he paid close attention to parts that wouldn't

24
00:02:18.043 --> 00:02:19.816
even be seen, like their backs.

25
00:02:20.877 --> 00:02:26.038
Later at Apple, I paid close attention to the design of each product too, making sure that everything

26
00:02:26.078 --> 00:02:27.998
looked sleek and was truly well made.

27
00:02:29.399 --> 00:02:35.941
I've heard that you had a wide range of interests in school—literature, philosophy, and music.

28
00:02:36.741 --> 00:02:39.301
How did you become interested in technology?

29
00:02:40.562 --> 00:02:44.163
Even in the 1960s, Silicon Valley was a big technology center.

30
00:02:45.103 --> 00:02:48.424
The Hewlett-Packard Company was already there making electronic devices.

31
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:54.501
Plus, I went to school with kids whose parents were engineers, and my friends were really into electronics.

32
00:02:54.982 --> 00:02:59.103
Do you know Steve Wozniak, who— Of course, I know Woz.

33
00:02:59.623 --> 00:03:01.923
He was your co-founder at Apple, right?

34
00:03:02.684 --> 00:03:04.004
Yes, but that came later.

35
00:03:04.664 --> 00:03:09.385
Woz was already a genius when it came to electronics, and the two of us liked playing pranks.

36
00:03:10.386 --> 00:03:15.127
Once Woz made a device that interrupted TV signals, which used to frustrate people so much!

37
00:03:15.827 --> 00:03:18.388
Eventually we figured out how to make a blue box too.

38
00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:23.973
This tricked phone lines into making expensive long-distance calls for free, illegally.

39
00:03:30.275 --> 00:03:32.616
Those devices sound pretty complicated.

40
00:03:32.636 --> 00:03:34.536
How old were you at the time?

41
00:03:35.437 --> 00:03:37.117
I was in my senior year of high school.

42
00:03:37.657 --> 00:03:41.318
After high school, I tried going to college but dropped out after a semester.

43
00:03:41.778 --> 00:03:43.459
I didn't like taking the required courses.

44
00:03:44.459 --> 00:03:47.440
Eventually I moved back home and got a job at a video game company.

45
00:03:48.252 --> 00:03:51.094
I also joined a club with Woz for computer hobbyists.

46
00:03:51.674 --> 00:03:56.517
By now Woz had designed his own computer, and I realized it could sell really well.

47
00:03:57.397 --> 00:04:03.360
This machine became the Apple I, and in 1976 we founded the Apple Computer Company in my parents'

48
00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:08.823
garage. We made two hundred Apple I computers and sold most of them within ten months.

49
00:04:10.144 --> 00:04:12.165
What was special about the Apple I?

50
00:04:12.866 --> 00:04:15.507
I mean, it doesn't look anything like a computer.

51
00:04:15.907 --> 00:04:16.768
Where's the monitor?

52
00:04:18.032 --> 00:04:21.415
Actually, no computers at the time had monitors, as you know them today.

53
00:04:22.376 --> 00:04:27.380
With our Apple I, we just sold the computer, and people could use their own TVs for monitors.

54
00:04:28.201 --> 00:04:30.583
Soon Woz started working on the Apple II.

55
00:04:30.963 --> 00:04:35.247
The Apple II was one of the first home computers, and when we started selling it, our company

56
00:04:35.287 --> 00:04:35.728
took off.

57
00:04:36.708 --> 00:04:41.953
In the early 80s, I developed the Macintosh, a great new computer that showed pictures onscreen and used

58
00:04:41.993 --> 00:04:42.333
a mouse.

59
00:04:43.054 --> 00:04:45.296
This technology was all pretty new at the time.

60
00:04:46.704 --> 00:04:48.765
I bet it was a huge success!

61
00:04:49.925 --> 00:04:53.126
It wasn't a failure, but it wasn't the success we had hoped for.

62
00:04:53.606 --> 00:04:58.227
By then other computers were available, and Apple lost its share of the market to competitors.

63
00:04:59.028 --> 00:05:02.188
The board of directors had grown unhappy with my performance as well.

64
00:05:02.649 --> 00:05:05.109
I left Apple and started another company called NeXT.

65
00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:11.571
Wow. How did it feel to leave Apple—a company that you founded?

66
00:05:11.971 --> 00:05:14.252
You must have felt angry and betrayed.

67
00:05:15.504 --> 00:05:18.865
I was upset but later realized that I benefited from the change.

68
00:05:19.526 --> 00:05:23.967
I was young when Apple started, and I became rich and famous quickly—too quickly.

69
00:05:24.948 --> 00:05:28.929
At NeXT I was able to start over, learn new lessons, and make new mistakes.

70
00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:35.812
Meanwhile the NeXT computers were too expensive, but the technology was exciting, and we were developing software that

71
00:05:35.952 --> 00:05:36.533
Apple wanted.

72
00:05:37.033 --> 00:05:39.914
So Apple bought NeXT, and soon I was back at Apple.

73
00:05:40.874 --> 00:05:43.935
Apple wasn't very profitable when you returned in 1997.

74
00:05:45.428 --> 00:05:48.570
In fact, many people were expecting it to shut down.

75
00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:53.252
But it grew into one of the most successful companies in the world.

76
00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:55.874
How did you ever turn things around?

77
00:05:56.994 --> 00:06:01.257
When I went back to Apple, the company was working on a lot of different projects.

78
00:06:03.798 --> 00:06:05.339
There wasn't much focus or vision.

79
00:06:06.179 --> 00:06:09.721
I decided we should work on a few core projects that we could do well.

80
00:06:10.882 --> 00:06:15.004
In 1997 only 37 percent of households in the US had a computer.

81
00:06:15.392 --> 00:06:17.574
There was also a new technology called the internet.

82
00:06:18.114 --> 00:06:22.918
How could we make both computers and the internet easier for people to use?

83
00:06:23.019 --> 00:06:26.722
In 1998 we released our answer to that question: the iMac.

84
00:06:27.262 --> 00:06:30.985
It was an all-in-one computer, ready to use almost right out of the box.

85
00:06:31.786 --> 00:06:35.589
And it had a friendly design that made technology less scary for many people.

86
00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:42.695
Within five months we sold 800,000 of them, and 32 percent of those buyers were purchasing a computer

87
00:06:42.755 --> 00:06:43.516
for the first time.

88
00:06:44.648 --> 00:06:48.312
Obviously, your early work at Apple was ahead of its time.

89
00:06:48.832 --> 00:06:55.298
But by the 2000s, you were in the right place at the right time, producing innovative new products.

90
00:06:56.879 --> 00:07:00.562
And now people like me can't imagine life without them.

91
00:07:02.584 --> 00:07:04.246
Thank you for being here, Steve.

92
00:07:04.746 --> 00:07:06.808
We'll let you get back to history now.

93
00:07:07.729 --> 00:07:18.539
Good-bye! And thank you, everyone, for watching my exclusive interview with Steve Jobs.

94
00:07:19.119 --> 00:07:24.462
As you might know, the last major product he launched at Apple was the iPad in 2010.

95
00:07:24.522 --> 00:07:28.344
He had cancer at the time, but he kept on working.

96
00:07:29.045 --> 00:07:35.088
Finally he became too ill to work and resigned from his day-to-day duties in 2011.

97
00:07:35.568 --> 00:07:39.770
Sadly, Steve Jobs died on October 5 of that year.

98
00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:46.906
He was only 56 years old, but he certainly made a huge impact on technology, as well as

99
00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:48.006
our everyday lives.

100
00:07:48.966 --> 00:07:53.168
That's all for today, but I'll be back soon with another exciting interview.

101
00:07:53.888 --> 00:08:01.071
You've been watching Faye Moss, the talk show host with exclusive access to history's biggest stars!
